  "reason": "insufficient_evidence",
  "statement": "Neither RNPS1 nor LPIN2 demonstrate sufficient functional or genetic evidence to causally link them to Alzheimer's disease pathology in this context. While LPIN2 has been observed in broad transcriptomic analyses of the AD brain and peripheral blood, it currently lacks any recorded genetic association with the disease. Similarly, RNPS1 appears only in high-throughput proteomic tau proximity assays or as a stable assay reference gene, and its Open Targets association score of 0.01 rests entirely on text-mining. Furthermore, STRING network analysis confirms that neither gene interacts with the core microglial and lipid-metabolism network formed by the other targets in this set.",
  "support": "grounded",
  "what_would_change_this": "Targeted in vivo mechanistic studies demonstrating that the disruption of RNPS1 or LPIN2 directly alters microglial behavior, myelination, or tau/amyloid pathology, alongside genome-wide significant associations linking coding variants in these genes to AD risk."
